---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import HBar from '../../components/charts/HBar.astro';
import BudgetSweep from '../../components/charts/BudgetSweep.astro';
import PromptProgression from '../../components/charts/PromptProgression.astro';
import {
  headline, setup, budgetSweep, promptProgression, forcedSkew,
  levers, droppedSft, limitations, nextSteps, codeExcerpt, codeSource,
} from '../../data/qwen';
---

<CaseLayout
  slug="qwen-4b"
  sources={['project_brief.md', 'old_qwen-4b_slide_deck.pdf', 'qwen-4b-inference.py', 'qwen-4b_kaggle_overview.md']}
  description="Pushing a fixed Qwen3-4B-Thinking model from 0.388 to 0.724 on a math-reasoning leaderboard with prompting and decoding alone, and no training, on a single 12 GB GPU."
>
  <Callout variant="tldr" label="TL;DR">
    The task fixed almost everything: a mandated <strong>Qwen3-4B-Thinking</strong>
    model, a single 12&nbsp;GB GPU, a strict exact-match grader, and no training
    allowed. So I treated inference itself as the problem to optimize. By budgeting
    the model's thinking, aligning the prompt to the grader, and tuning the decoding,
    the leaderboard score went from a <strong>0.388 baseline to 0.724</strong>, and
    every gain came from those inference-time levers rather than a bigger model.
  </Callout>

  <Figure title="Leaderboard score: baseline → best" source="deck" scale="0–1 · 943 private q"
    caption="Best of two runs. The jump comes entirely from prompting and decoding; the weights never changed.">
    <HBar
      ariaLabel="Leaderboard score rose from a 0.388 baseline to 0.724, a gain of 0.336 with zero training."
      decimals={3}
      max={0.8}
      data={[
        { label: 'Baseline', value: headline.baseline, role: 'baseline' },
        { label: 'Best run', value: headline.best, role: 'measured', note: `+${headline.delta}` },
      ]}
    />
    <DataTable slot="table" caption="Leaderboard score, baseline versus best run."
      columns={['Run', 'Leaderboard score']} numCols={[1]}
      rows={[['Baseline', headline.baseline.toFixed(3)], ['Best run', headline.best.toFixed(3)]]} />
  </Figure>

  <section class="case-section">
    <span class="eyebrow">The problem</span>
    <h2>Getting everything out of a model I could not retrain.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The task fixed almost everything: the model ({setup.model}), the hardware
      ({setup.gpu}), and a grader that accepts an answer only on an exact string
      match. The only free variables were how the model thinks and how it decodes.
    </p>
    <p>
      That reframes the whole project as an <strong>inference-time optimization</strong>.
      The goal was not to train something better, but to get every last point out of
      weights I was not allowed to touch. The dataset spans high-school to graduate
      math, with {setup.publicQ.toLocaleString()} public questions for development and
      {' '}{setup.privateQ} private questions for the graded leaderboard, in a mix of
      free-form (exact value) and multiple-choice. There were no external data, tools,
      or calculators; all weights came from HuggingFace, served with {setup.serving}.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Callout variant="finding" label="Read the axes carefully: two different scales">
    Results live on <strong>two scales that are never comparable</strong>, and they
    never share an axis on this page.
    <br /><br />
    <strong>Dev accuracy (%)</strong> is the percent correct on the {setup.devQ}-question
    public dev set, a fast probe. <strong>Leaderboard score (0–1)</strong> is the
    official graded score over all {setup.privateQ} private questions. The headline
    <strong>0.724 is a leaderboard score.</strong>
  </Callout>

  <section class="case-section">
    <span class="eyebrow">The approach · four levers</span>
    <h2>Four levers, one decisive.</h2>
    <div class="levers">
      {
        levers.map((l) => (
          <div class:list={['lever', { 'lever--decisive': l.decisive }]}>
            <div class="lever__head">
              <span class="lever__n mono">{String(l.n).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              {l.decisive && <span class="lever__badge mono">decisive</span>}
            </div>
            <h3 class="lever__title">{l.title}</h3>
            <p class="lever__body">{l.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <span class="eyebrow">Results · Experiment 1</span>
    <h2>How much thinking is too much?</h2>
    <p>
      Sweeping the thinking-token budget showed a clear optimum: with too little, the
      model cannot finish reasoning, and with too much, it wanders past the answer and
      gets truncated. <strong>12,288 max-tokens with a 9,000-token budget</strong> was the peak.
    </p>
    <Figure title="Thinking-budget sweep" source={budgetSweep.source} scale={budgetSweep.scale}
      caption="Dev accuracy against the token budget. The optimum sits in the middle, since more thinking is not better.">
      <BudgetSweep />
      <DataTable slot="table" caption="Dev accuracy by thinking budget."
        columns={['max-tokens / budget', 'Dev accuracy']} numCols={[1]}
        rows={budgetSweep.points.map((p) => [p.label, p.devAcc + '%'])} />
    </Figure>

    <span class="eyebrow" style="display:block; margin-top: var(--space-7);">Results · Experiment 2</span>
    <h2>The prompt was the lever that mattered.</h2>
    <p>
      Rewriting the system prompt to speak the grader's language, with plain ASCII, at
      least 12 significant figures, and brackets preserved, moved the leaderboard score
      more than anything else. Light sampling on top of it compounded the gain.
    </p>
    <Figure title="Prompt engineering progression" source={promptProgression.source} scale={promptProgression.scale}
      caption="Greedy versus temperature 0.6, across two prompt generations. Gains are small but real and stack.">
      <PromptProgression />
      <DataTable slot="table" caption="Leaderboard score by prompt and decoding."
        columns={['Prompt', 'Greedy', 'Temp 0.6']} numCols={[1, 2]}
        rows={promptProgression.series.map((s) => [s.prompt, s.greedy.toFixed(3), s.temp06.toFixed(3)])} />
    </Figure>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <span class="eyebrow">The key finding</span>
    <h2>Most misses were formatting, not reasoning.</h2>
    <p>
      When questions were forced to answer at the budget cap, the error pattern was
      lopsided: multiple-choice answers held up, but free-form answers collapsed, not
      because the math was wrong, but because the output did not match the grader's
      exact format.
    </p>
    <Figure title="Forced-to-answer correctness" source={forcedSkew.source} scale={forcedSkew.scale}
      caption="Multiple-choice versus free-form, when forced to answer. The gap is a formatting gap, not a reasoning gap.">
      <HBar
        ariaLabel="When forced to answer, multiple-choice was 81% correct versus 30% for free-form."
        suffix="%"
        max={100}
        data={[
          { label: 'Multiple-choice', value: forcedSkew.rows[0].correctPct, role: 'measured' },
          { label: 'Free-form', value: forcedSkew.rows[1].correctPct, role: 'human' },
        ]}
      />
      <DataTable slot="table" caption="Correctness when forced to answer, by question type."
        columns={['Question type', 'Correct']} numCols={[1]}
        rows={forcedSkew.rows.map((r) => [r.kind, r.correctPct + '%'])} />
    </Figure>
    <Callout variant="finding" label="Why this mattered">
      <strong>~80% of free-form misses were formatting or truncation, not bad math.</strong>
      So the effort went where the points were, into format discipline and budget,
      rather than toward a bigger model it was not allowed to use.
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <span class="eyebrow">The engineering</span>
    <h2>The winning configuration, verbatim.</h2>
    <p>
      The two-phase decoder is the heart of it: think up to the budget, and if the
      model has not closed <code>&lt;/think&gt;</code> on its own, inject a forced
      close and spend the remaining tokens stating a boxed answer.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={codeExcerpt} filename={codeSource} lang="python" />
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <span class="eyebrow">Honest limitations</span>
    <h2>What this is not.</h2>
    <Callout variant="caution" label="A path tried and dropped">
      Supervised fine-tuning <strong>was attempted and abandoned</strong>: it
      collapsed dev accuracy from <strong>{droppedSft.before}% to {droppedSft.after}%</strong>.
      The model was kept intact, and knowing when to stop was part of the result.
    </Callout>
    <ul class="prose-list">
      {limitations.map((l) => <li>{l}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <h3>Where it goes next</h3>
    <ul class="prose-list">
      {nextSteps.map((s) => <li>{s}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>

<style>
  .levers { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); margin-top: var(--space-5); }
  @media (min-width: 40rem) { .levers { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .lever {
    border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-5); background: var(--bg);
  }
  .lever--decisive { border-color: var(--cobalt-line); background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--cobalt-soft) 30%, var(--bg)); }
  .lever__head { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  .lever__n { font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--muted); }
  .lever__badge {
    font-size: var(--step--2); color: var(--cobalt-ink); background: var(--cobalt-soft);
    padding: 0.1rem 0.45rem; border-radius: 3px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }
  .lever__title { margin: 0; font-size: var(--step-1); letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .lever__body { margin-top: 0.5rem; font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--ink); line-height: 1.55; }
</style>
